instead of developers accessing your  backend services directly  they can be exposed through api proxies  on apigee  which are http endpoints the proxy  manages any code changes on the backend  and avoids disruptions for developers  until a new version of the api is  warranted  an api proxy enables you to apply  security features  enforce access limits and quotas provide  caching  create transformations and handle errors  it also lets you perform monitoring  and create analytic reports you usually  group  multiple api proxies into an api product  and  offer that as a service plan for example  you may want to provide an api product  for free up to a certain request limit  per day for developers to try out your  api  but we'll cover that in another episode  you will have  some flexibility when creating your api  proxy and so  to get started in this episode i will  walk you through how to build your first  api proxy  [Music]  before getting started you will need to  have configured access to your api proxy  either externally  or internally i have linked instructions  in this video  called accessing api proxies  next visit the apigee console at  apigee.google.com  then develop api proxies  then select create new this will prompt  the proxy wizard  and you will choose reverse proxy  next enter the following values for the  proxy name proxy base path  description and the target existing api  then click next and apigee will create  your proxy  next choose to visit the api proxy list  so that we can deploy  our proxy by returning to develop  api proxies again next ensure you are  on your eval environment by looking at  the top otherwise  use the drop down to change environments  click on my proxy then notice that under  deployments the revision column shows  not deployed next from the revision drop  down choose one  as this is the first version and then  click deploy  after a few seconds pass you can see  from the status bar  when your proxy is fully deployed  now you can test the api proxy by using  the command line terminal  as a friendly reminder if you haven't  configured access to your proxies either  internally or externally i have linked  instructions  on how to do that note that in my case  the external ip provided by an external  load balancer  is registered with a dns registered  hostname  next if the call succeeds you will see  the hello guest has the output now let's  try changing the target  endpoint to output xml so let's return  to develop  api proxies again select your desired  proxy  then on the top far right click develop  which will take you to the api proxy  editor  which lets you see the structure of your  api proxy and configure its flow  in the navigator pane select target  endpoints  preflow on the code pane you can spot  your target url  endpoint next append forward slash xml  to the existing url  so that it looks like this next click  save  you'll be asked to save it as a new  revision since you already deployed  revision 1  and that can't be changed then deploy to  the new revision by selecting the button  deploy to eval and choose deploy to  test your changes by sending a request  to your proxy via command line  just like we did earlier like this and  this should return xml  now let's add a policy to convert that  xml into json  we will use an xml to json policy in the  proxy response flow  this policy converts the payload of an  xml message  to json it also changes the response's  content type header  to add the xml to json policy to your  proxy let's return to api  proxies develop  select your desired proxy then on the  top far right click develop  this time click proxy endpoints  default preflow to add a policy to your  proxy  click the plus step button in the  response preflow  the bottom half of the flow editor  apogee displays a categorized list of  policies in the ad  dialog box that you can add to your flow  scroll down and select  the xml to json policy leave the default  names and click add  apigee attaches the new policy to the  pre-flow of the response which does  three things  one it adds the new policy under  policies in the navigator pane  two it adds the xml to json policy in  the flow pane  and three it displays the policies  configuration xml in the code pane  we'll just use the default configuration  that's suggested but this can be  customized  next click save and test it out with the  following curl command  congrats you've successfully added the  xml to json policy to your new proxy  as the next step i have linked in this  video's description  more information on how to add  additional functionality to your api  proxies  if you're interested in the next episode  i will walk you through  how to apply apogee security policies to  your proxy  so stay tuned and friends if you found  this episode helpful  you can click like or subscribe to the  channel cheers  [Music]
 

